WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.665
Atomic operations come in a few common families.

2
00:00:02.665 --> 00:00:05.663
Loads read the current value of an atomic object.

3
00:00:05.663 --> 00:00:08.329
Stores publish a new value to that object.

4
00:00:08.329 --> 00:00:11.993
These operations are the foundation for simple flags and status values.

5
00:00:11.993 --> 00:00:17.323
Exchange replaces the value and returns the previous value as one atomic operation for that object.

6
00:00:17.323 --> 00:00:24.986
It can be useful when code needs to claim or reset a simple state while learning what state was present before the change.

7
00:00:24.986 --> 00:00:29.650
Fetch-add and fetch-sub style operations support counters and similar independent numeric updates.

8
00:00:29.650 --> 00:00:37.312
They are useful for telemetry, simple reference-like counts, and sequence numbers, but the meaning of the count still needs a clear policy.

9
00:00:37.312 --> 00:00:39.644
Compare-and-exchange is more review-sensitive.

10
00:00:39.644 --> 00:00:45.307
It checks whether the atomic currently has an expected value, and if so, writes a desired value.

11
00:00:45.307 --> 00:00:51.970
If the value is different, the expected value may be updated and the caller must decide what to do next.

12
00:00:51.970 --> 00:00:58.633
Loops around compare-and-exchange need careful review because retry behavior can hide assumptions about progress, valid states, and contention.

13
00:00:58.633 --> 00:01:03.963
This course keeps that discussion at a conceptual level rather than building advanced lock-free structures.

14
00:01:03.963 --> 00:01:09.294
Read-modify-write operations are atomic for the target object, not for the surrounding business logic.

15
00:01:09.294 --> 00:01:16.290
If a transition also depends on a container, pointer, timestamp, or object lifetime, additional synchronization or design structure may still be

16
00:01:16.290 --> 00:01:16.623
required.

17
00:01:16.623 --> 00:01:18.622
Good atomic operation use is readable.

18
00:01:18.622 --> 00:01:26.284
The operation should match the state being represented, and reviewers should be able to tell why a load, store, exchange, fetch operation, or

19
00:01:26.284 --> 00:01:27.950
compare-and-exchange was chosen.

20
00:01:27.950 --> 00:01:33.280
When that choice is not obvious, prefer a named helper or a more explicit synchronization boundary.
